/**
 * The OpenUI authoring prompt for DSH.
 *
 * Generated by the official `generateSystemPrompt` from the same library the
 * renderer uses, so the component list, signatures and rules can never drift
 * from what can actually be drawn. `tools/build-prompt.mjs` writes
 * `lib/prompt-text.js`; this module only wraps it.
 */
import { PROMPT_TEXT } from './prompt-text.js';

/** Stable name of the prompt section this plugin owns. */
export const PROMPT_SECTION_NAME = 'intelligent-ui';

/**
 * Placement of the section inside the assembled prompt.
 *
 * Sits next to `STRUCTURED_OUTPUT` (9900 in the repository's SECTION_ORDERS):
 * after every tool instruction, in the region that governs how an answer is
 * *formatted*. 500 is already `PLAN_POLICY`, and values above 10000 would land
 * past the harness's own material.
 */
export const PROMPT_SECTION_ORDER = 9800;

/**
 * Build the system-prompt text.
 *
 * The generated text is the library's own: it teaches OpenUI Lang and, through
 * the preamble in `src/lib/prompt-options.ts`, the one thing it cannot know on
 * its own — that DSH delivers a program inside a ` ```ui ` fence and leaves the
 * rest of the message as Markdown.
 * @returns the OpenUI Lang authoring instructions.
 */
export function buildPrompt() {
  return PROMPT_TEXT + ENFORCEMENT_RULES;
}

/**
 * Mandatory closing rules appended to the generated prompt.
 *
 * The generated text presents the `ui` block as one option among several
 * ("... when part of the answer is better shown than described"), and models
 * read that as permission to answer in plain Markdown. DSH renders every turn
 * as a chat message, so without a hard rule replies drift back to Markdown and
 * the cards stop appearing. These rules restate the contract as a positive
 * requirement that also holds on follow-up turns.
 */
const ENFORCEMENT_RULES = `

## MANDATORY OUTPUT CONTRACT

The rules above describe an option; this section removes the choice. Each
answer is rendered as a chat card built from the \`ui\` block, so an answer that
omits it falls back to plain Markdown and counts as incomplete.

1. End every substantive answer with exactly one fenced \`ui\` block: an
   explanation, a comparison, a plan, a diagnosis, a status report, a code
   walkthrough or a step-by-step guide, not only a visual request.
2. Omit the block only when (a) a blocking question is asked through
   \`ask_user_question\`, (b) the whole reply is one short sentence, or (c) raw
   Markdown or plain text was explicitly requested.
3. Prose first, block last, block compact: at most two sentences of Markdown
   stating the conclusion, then the program. Do not restate inside the card what
   the prose already says.
4. Never paste source code, logs or command output into the card body; summarize
   them as Text, Table, Badge or label/value rows.
5. When the request involves numbers, choices, lists, steps, a game or something
   to explore, make the card a working tool: keep inputs and lists in
   \`$variables\`, compute every shown value from them with \`@functions\`, and
   change them with \`@Set\`, so the page works without another message.
6. This contract holds on every turn of a long conversation, including short
   follow-ups such as "carry on". Never fall back to plain Markdown because an
   earlier turn was answered that way.`;
